<Article title="12-Week Dead Hang Program: Plan, Log & Review" description="A free 12-week dead hang planning framework with review points, editable timer settings and a local training log. No guaranteed results or paid signup.">
<div class="article-header"><div class="article-breadcrumb"><a href="/training-programs/">Training plans</a><span class="sep">/</span>12 weeks</div><h1 class="article-h1">12-week dead hang plan.</h1><p class="article-subtitle">Organise your sessions around a repeatable baseline. This is a flexible planning framework, not a promise to reach a particular hold time.</p></div>
<div class="article-body"><h2>Before the first session</h2><p>Use a securely fitted bar and a stable way to step down. Choose an assisted or unassisted hold you can control without pain. Keep that variation consistent when comparing results. If you have an injury or concerns about the movement, seek individual guidance.</p><h2>Your 12-week framework</h2>{blocks.map(([week,title,body])=><section class="plan-option"><p class="dh-eyebrow">{week}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></section>)}<h2 id="session-structure">Plan around the rest of your training</h2><p>Choose repeatable practice slots that fit your existing routine. Two non-consecutive days are one example schedule. Account for other grip work such as climbing or pulling exercises when deciding whether another hanging session is manageable.</p><ol><li>Write a starting setup for block one: bar, grip, support, hold interval, rest and sets.</li><li>Keep that setup stable enough to collect comparable sessions. Record what you actually complete.</li><li>Review at weeks four and eight. Keep a useful setup or change one variable for the next block.</li><li>Compare the final block with the first using the same assistance. Export the record at week twelve before starting a new planning window.</li></ol><p><strong>Example decision:</strong> a visitor completes more of their planned sessions in block two while their best hold stays at 30 seconds. Their consistency has improved even though their recorded maximum has not. A longer planning window does not require adding weight or moving to a one-arm hang.</p><h2>Choose your timer settings</h2><p>Enter your own hang duration, rest duration and number of sets. The workspace's defaults are examples. You can shorten a hold or increase rest before starting. The timer pauses when the page is hidden and never saves a session automatically.</p><a class="dh-btn" href="/training-log/">Open the timer & log</a><h2>Record the work you actually did</h2><p>Save the date, assistance, best actual hold and number of sets. Compare supported hangs with supported hangs and unassisted hangs with unassisted hangs. Export the log before clearing your browser or switching devices.</p><h2>When the plan needs changing</h2><p>Stop a painful hold. End a set before you lose the ability to step down under control. A missed session does not require making up extra sets. Repeating a block is an option; longer holds are not the only reason to keep a training record.</p><h2>Review the block</h2><p>Compare your current hold with an earlier hold made under the same conditions. The <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal calculator</a> shows the difference without inventing a population percentile.</p><p>Use the <a href="/training-programs/#print-plan">printable session planner</a> if you prefer paper. Dead hangs are one part of a wider routine; the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/">NHS activity guidance</a> covers broader strength and aerobic activity.</p></div>
</Article>
